## Migration: Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Classification

Your code contains:
| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Cleanup on cancellation | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` in Stop | Timeout before force-kill | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` |

### Why This Fails on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it cannot forcibly terminate a thread. You must switch to **cooperative cancellation** where the worker voluntarily checks a `CancellationToken`.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock waits
                token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (replaces ThreadAbortException catch)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just continue to exit the loop naturally
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative shutdown

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for graceful exit
        if (_workerThread is not null && _workerThread.IsAlive)
        {
            if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
            {
                // Thread didn't stop — it's stuck in uncooperative code.
                // Log a warning. You cannot force-kill it in modern .NET.
                Console.WriteLine("Warning: worker thread did not exit within timeout.");
            }
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Critical Changes Explained

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `while (_running)` | `while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)` | Token is thread-safe; no need for `volatile bool` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` | The `when` filter avoids catching unrelated OCEs |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | *(removed)* | Loop naturally continues or exits based on token state |
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Cooperative signal; no `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` | `_workerThread.Join(2000)` | Actually waits for thread exit instead of sleeping blindly |

---

### Key Pitfall: `GetNextItem()` Must Accept the Token

If `GetNextItem()` blocks (e.g., on a queue or I/O), **cancellation won't take effect** until it unblocks. You must thread the token through:

```csharp
// If using BlockingCollection<T>:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
}

// If using a custom wait:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    // Replace Thread.Sleep or WaitOne with token-aware wait
    token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(pollInterval);
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    return DequeueItem();
}
```

---

### Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| Not passing token to blocking calls (`GetNextItem`) | Thread hangs forever; `Stop()` never completes |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` | Timer/callback leaks |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` without the `when` filter | Accidentally swallows cancellations from unrelated subsystems |
| Assuming `Cancel()` is immediate | It only works at the next checkpoint — insert `ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive operations |
| Using `Thread.Interrupt()` as a fallback | Also obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9); use token signaling instead |
